ation: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nQ: 1, J: 1, 10: 2, 9: 2, 7: 2, 6: 1\nValid Moves:\nsingle: ♥3\nsingle: ♦Q\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '**My thought process starts with a clear understanding of the situation: I\'m a Peasant, it\'s my turn with a "Free Play," and I\'ve got a 3 of Hearts and a Queen of Diamonds.**  The key information I have is that the "Cards Remaining Unknown" list tells me what\'s *left* in the deck, and importantly, it *doesn\'t* list any Kings, Aces, Twos, or Jokers. That\'s huge!  If those cards aren\'t listed, it means they *aren\'t* in the remaining deck.\n\n**Immediately, I recognize that this changes everything.** Because I hold a Queen, and the list only contains a single Queen as unknown, and no higher cards like K, A, or 2, my Queen is effectively the "highest card" or the *nuts*. I\'ve got to use this to my advantage.\n\n**The most logical move is to play the Queen.**  If I play the Queen, nobody can beat it.  I\'ll win the trick and have control of the next play. That guarantees I can play my 3 of Hearts and be finished. The goal is to finish the game. If I play the Queen, I have a clear path to victory.\n\n**I have to consider the alternative:** Playing the 3 first. If I play the 3, I give up control. The Landlord might have a higher card to play, maybe even the other Queen. The other Queen would mean I lose control, and the Landlord might win the game.\n\n**This is a straightforward game where I can win.** It\'s about securing that win. The goal is to get all my cards out or help my teammate get all their cards out. Since I have a way to get out, I will take it.\n\n**I keep checking the rules.** The game ends when any peasant gets rid of their cards.\n\n**Evaluation:** My remaining cards and the state of the game make this a simple play. I can guarantee a win.\n\n**Reasoning:** Playing the Queen gives me absolute control and a guaranteed win on the trick, letting me finish the game on my next turn.\n\n**The final decision:** I\'m playing the Queen of Diamonds.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: Based on the card counting, the highest remaining cards in the deck are Queens (Q), as all Kings, Aces, 2s, and Jokers are exhausted (not listed in the unknown cards). Furthermore, no bombs are possible since the maximum count for any remaining rank is 2. This makes the ♦Q in my hand the highest possible card (the "nuts").\n\nReason: By playing the ♦Q now, I am guaranteed to win the trick since no one can beat it. This will give me priority (a free play) for the next turn, at which point I can play my final card (♥3) to empty my hand. This sequence forces an immediate win for the Peasant team. Playing the ♥3 first would be risky, as it would allow the Landlord to potentially win the trick (e.g., with the other Q) and dictate the game flow.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦Q"]}'}
